 "instructions": "Translate every \"en\" string into Simplified Chinese (zh-Hans), game-guide register. Follow glossary.json strictly. Keep ⟦0⟧-style placeholders, numbers, units and wiki markup exactly. Return only {\"items\":[{\"k\":\"...\",\"zh\":\"...\"}]} with the same k values.",
 "units": [
  {
   "k": "1ahrbys",
   "en": "Pre-conference measures"
  },
  {
   "k": "1861k7w",
   "en": "AI victors can be unpredictable in contests and AI puppets are particularly wasteful of war score. If an AI ally is not essential for defeating an enemy, keeping them out of the war will go a long way toward giving the player more control of the conference and ensure all states are properly dispositioned (i.e., taken, puppeted, etc.)."
  },
  {
   "k": "1am9p6k",
   "en": "Land combat damage and occupation tend to be the largest factors in war participation gain. Becoming proficient in combat will have a second-order effect of increasing war score in these areas. Naval and air combat are also excellent contributors for countries that can field an adequate navy and air force. Lend lease tends give the least return on investment and should be avoided when the materiel could be put to better use in the player’s military."
  },
  {
   "k": "1fihel",
   "en": "For non-democratic nations, collaboration governments created through the espionage mechanics allow the player to take advantage of the Compliance cost modifier. This often requires performing two collaboration missions, so the player must weigh their cost versus benefit."
  },
  {
   "k": "1wslqxx",
   "en": "Priorities"
  },
  {
   "k": "1jyd9zp",
   "en": "Players should generally prioritize deciding the fate of states before selecting naval vessels and other demands. This is because the total war score distribution computed at the beginning of the conference is based on state value and does not account for naval vessels or the value of additional demands such as DMZs, war reparations, and resources. Even in a single-victor conference where there is no AI to contend with, the player may not have sufficient war score to disposition all states and take all naval vessels, particularly if the loser has a large navy."
  },
  {
   "k": "11y8pwd",
   "en": "If multiple contests erupt with the AI, it is worth sticking to demands for only one or two turns. This is because the player will start permanently losing some war score after the first turn of the contest and will incur opportunity costs on demanding other states. When there are multiple states contested, the AI will start forfeiting some of their demands at a rate of 1 or 2 per turn, but eventually the AI will stubbornly stick to a few demands and the player will face diminishing returns fighting for them."
  },
  {
   "k": "75dems",
   "en": "Leveraging cost modifiers"
  },
  {
   "k": "9sj681",
   "en": "The player may take advantage of two powerful cost modifiers during the conference to decrease cumulative demand costs:"
  },
  {
   "k": "loi6jh",
   "en": "Continuous political action – Once the player makes a puppet or force government demand on a loser, all puppet and force government demands on the same loser will be 15% cheaper on subsequent turns. This allows the player to puppet or force one cheap state on one turn, and then get discounts on more expensive states of the losing country on subsequent turns."
  },
  {
   "k": "1inrj6m",
   "en": "Influence – Demands on a state are cheaper when the player has control of states nearby, especially if they are on the border; this includes states the player successfully annexes during the conference regardless of how much they cost. This allows a player to annex a cheap state on one turn, and get a discount on more expensive states nearby on subsequent turns."
  },
  {
   "k": "jdd66h",
   "en": "The effects of both modifiers are stackable for puppeting/forcing government actions. The player may, for example, annex a cheap state and puppet another cheap state on turn one, and then puppet additional states on subsequent turns with both modifiers multiplied by each other for a larger discount (the annexed state may be transferred to the puppet after the conference)."
  },
  {
   "k": "1jahc9x",
   "en": "For single-victor conferences, these techniques will free up more war score for other demands (take navy, reparations, etc.). With no pressure from other victors, the player can use influence from annexations over a number of turns to work their way around the country, from cheapest to most expensive states, with significant cumulative savings by the time the last state is annexed."
  },
  {
   "k": "l1iav7",
   "en": "For multi-victor conferences, the player can still benefit from these techniques but must balance the number of turns required to take advantage of it with the pressure that will come from competing victors."
  },
  {
   "k": "1p1yprf",
   "en": "References"
